  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways of storing solar energy and highlights a large difference in energy density. On the left, sunlight is captured by a solar panel and stored in a lithium-ion battery. On the right, sunlight is captured by a plant and converted into a liquid fuel or bio-based chemical stored in a bottle. The central message is that these two storage routes differ greatly in how much energy can be packed per unit mass.\n\nThe battery is labeled at roughly 0.30–0.8 MJ per kilogram, whereas the liquid product is labeled around 27–47 MJ per kilogram. A large stack of many battery icons is shown beneath the single bottle, visually emphasizing that it would take many lithium-ion batteries to store the same amount of energy as one bottle of fuel. The word “Energy =” next to the bottle reinforces this equivalence.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a simple comparison between electrochemical storage and biologically derived fuel storage: batteries are powered by solar electricity, while plants convert sunlight into energy-rich molecules, and those molecules appear far more energy-dense by weight.",
